  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "scriptId": "src-06-rationalisation-conjugates",
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Rationalisation and Conjugate Radical Forms",
    "description": "Remove irrational denominators with a matching conjugate and use conjugate pairs to make difficult surd expressions collapse.",
    "teachingApproach": "Begin with a single-root denominator, earn the conjugate idea from difference of squares, then handle root-root denominators, conjugate ratios, reciprocal pairs, unit-product conjugates, symmetric x/y pairs and a multi-term capstone. Every step asks what denominator we want after multiplication and uses that to choose the conjugate.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": "Teach rationalisation from the reason it works: conjugates multiply to a difference of squares. Begin with 1/√a, then denominators such as 2+√2 and √5−√3, followed by ratios such as (√5+√3)/(√5−√3). Cover reciprocal conjugate pairs, denominators whose conjugate product is 1, and the chapter pattern where two conjugate-defined quantities make x+y, x²+y² or a reciprocal expression simple. End with a short multi-term rationalisation capstone in the same style as the objective questions. Learners should choose the conjugate from the exact denominator and check that the new denominator is rational.",
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 5, Square Roots and Cube Roots. Approved scope: 31"
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's learn the one move that removes a square root from a denominator.\n\nThe trick is not magic: choose a partner that makes a difference of squares, so the denominator becomes an ordinary number.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A denominator like √5 can be removed by multiplying by the same root.\n\n1/√5 × √5/√5\n= √5/5",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "The value has not changed because we multiplied by 1.\n\nThe denominator is now rational: 5.\n\nThis is the simplest form of rationalisation.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "q_1": {
      "id": "q_1",
      "type": "CHOICE",
